## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to enforce tenancy isolation and data minimization in prompts and outputs, plus post-checks before delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented prompts, tenancy scoping, redaction, post-filtering, test harness concepts.

## Code Example
```javascript
function wrapPrompt(tenantId, userPrompt) {
  // basic tenant-scoped redaction and prefix
  const safePrompt = `[TENANT:${tenantId}] PROMPT: ${userPrompt}`;
  return safePrompt;
}

function mockLLM(prompt){
  // simulate a model that may leak data
  return `Response for ${prompt} with tenant id ${prompt.match(/TENANT:(\\w+)/)[1]}`;
}

function postCheck(response, tenantId){
  // naive leakage check
  if (response.includes(tenantId)) return null;
  return response;
}

console.log(postCheck(mockLLM(wrapPrompt('T123','List logs for user emails')),
  'T123'));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve this for real-world tenancy boundaries and performance at scale?
- What tests would you add to catch redaction misses and leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic redaction rules per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design a practical, testable clarifier routing mechanism, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing and simple scoring
- Signals: intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, conflicts, missing data
- Payload design for downstream modules

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny prototype
function chooseClarifier(signals){
  const {intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role} = signals;
  if (intent_confidence < 0.5) return 'request-region';
  if (data_availability.includes('period')) return 'request-period';
  if (user_role==='analytics') return 'request-metric';
  return 'request-period';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate clarifier quality in automated tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design stable prompts across models and languages with guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt contracts, per-model templates, and intent extraction
- Drift testing and edge-case handling for ambiguity
- Guardrails and multilingual normalization
- Minimal prototype demonstration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function routePrompt(userInput, context) {
  const templates = {
    en: { default: 'Template_EN' },
    fr: { default: 'Template_FR' }
  };
  const model = context.model || 'default';
  const lang = context.lang || 'en';
  const chosen = (templates[lang] && templates[lang].default) || templates['en'].default;
  const finalPrompt = chosen + '\n' + userInput;
  return { template: chosen, finalPrompt, guardrail: 'none' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-language behavior and detect prompt drift?
- How would you version templates and roll them out safely?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a modular, auditable routing layer for prompts with data-sensitivity and reproducibility concerns. It emphasizes concrete signals, edge-case handling, and a small prototype to demonstrate flow control.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_sensitivity, reproducibility
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, missing sources, conflicting data
- Lightweight prototyping: minimal module map and payload schema

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt) {
  const text = prompt.toLowerCase()
  const toLiterature = /(summarize|literature|research)/.test(text)
  const toExperiment = /(experiment|plan|propose)/.test(text)
  const needsAudit = /(audit|trace|reproducible)/.test(text)

  if (toLiterature && toExperiment && needsAudit) {
    return {
      modules: ['Literature-Summary', 'Experiment-Plan', 'Audit-Log-Formatter'],
      payload: { prompt, sources: [], trace: { confidence: 0.8 } }
    }
  }
  // Additional routing logic...
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical routing under legal constraints that change with jurisdiction, not just prompt quality.

## Key Concepts
- Jurisdiction-aware routing
- Guardrail layering across modules
- Conflict resolution and edge-case handling
- Minimal, testable prototype interfaces

## Code Example
```javascript
// simplified prototype in JS
function route(prompt, jurisdiction){
  const intent = /policy|policy\squery/i.test(prompt) ? 'policy_query' : 'content_action';
  const sensitive = /(SSN|PIN|email)/i.test(prompt);
  let guardrail = jurisdiction === 'GDPR' ? 'minimize personal data processing' : null;
  return { module: 'policy_auto', guardrail };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical prompt versioning and drift handling with concrete routing decisions for enterprise data assistants.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection via keyword overlap scoring
- Baseline prompt management with patch generation
- Three-tier routing: Intent-Detector, Safety-Guard, Response-Composer
- Signal tracking: intent, data_sensitivity, version_delta
- Edge case handling: renamed intents, missing baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for drift detection and routing
function routePrompt(newPrompt, baselinePrompt) {
  const overlap = calculateKeywordOverlap(newPrompt, baselinePrompt);
  const hasSafetyIssues = detectSafetyKeywords(newPrompt);

  if (hasSafetyIssues) return 'Safety-Guard';
  if (overlap < 0.6) return 'Intent-Detector';
  return 'Response-Composer';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate the overlap threshold for different use cases?
- What approach would you use to store patches for audit trails?
- How would you handle version conflicts when multiple teams modify prompts simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes system design for multi-tenant prompt governance with decomposition, guardrails, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt decomposition
- Tenant-specific policy enforcement
- Guardrail injection and leakage prevention
- Provenance and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype illustrating flow
function governanceFlow(request, policy){
  const subs = decompose(request);
  const guarded = subs.map(s => injectGuard(s, policy));
  const finalOut = assemble(guarded);
  const audit = {tenant: policy.tenant, ts: Date.now(), steps: subs.length};
  return {finalOut, audit};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate guardrails against adversarial prompts and policy drift?
- What metrics would you instrument to measure governance efficacy and latency?
